  m_Name: DancingGirl
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ARMuseum.Exhibits.ExhibitData
  title: DANCING GIRL OF MOHENJO-DARO
  selectorLabel: DANCING GIRL
  subtitle: "Bronze Figurine \xB7 Indus Valley Civilisation"
  category: "Indus Valley Civilisation \u2022 Bronze Figurine"
  description: 'A celebrated bronze figurine from the Indus Valley Civilisation, depicting a young woman standing confidently with one hand resting on her hip and the other hanging by her side.


    The figurine is associated with Mohenjo-daro, one of the major urban centres of the Indus Valley Civilisation, and is among the civilisation''s best-known artefacts.'
  symbolism: []
  historicalContext: The Dancing Girl is associated with the ancient city of Mohenjo-daro, one of the major urban centres of the Indus Valley Civilisation. The figurine was made using the lost-wax casting technique, demonstrating the metalworking skills of its makers.
  provenanceNote: This is a digital 3D model of a bronze figurine associated with Mohenjo-daro. The model is displayed at a viewing scale of 30 cm, which is not the original artefact's actual height. Museum attribution, findspot and acquisition details are not documented in this exhibit.
  narration: 'Welcome. This exhibit presents the Dancing Girl of Mohenjo-daro, a bronze figurine from the Indus Valley Civilisation.

    The figurine shows a young woman standing confidently, with one hand resting on her hip and the other hanging by her side.

    It is associated with Mohenjo-daro, one of the major urban centres of the Indus Valley Civilisation.

    The figurine was made using the lost-wax casting technique, which shows the metalworking skills of its makers.

    Its posture and ornaments have made it one of the civilisation''s best-known artefacts.

    The model is displayed at a viewing scale of 30 centimetres, which is not the original height of the artefact.

    Thank you for exploring this exhibit.'
  narrationClip: {fileID: 0}
